#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Hold a block of RAM so the instance is not reclaimed as idle.

This is a workaround, and worth naming as one. Oracle reclaims an Always Free
instance when, across a continuous 7-day window, CPU 95th percentile, network
AND memory utilisation are ALL under 20%. It is an AND, so lifting any single
one of the three is enough.

Measured on this box over six days of `sar` history, all three sat inside the
window. The interesting part is why, because it is not that the box does
nothing:

  - CPU p95 on a quiet day was 6.0%, and the daily MAX was 6.5%. The sampler
    runs 8x a day for ~11 minutes and barely registers, because it spends that
    time waiting on the game API rather than computing. An I/O-bound workload
    is invisible to a CPU metric.
  - Memory ran 20-21% with dips to 19.9% -- the threshold itself.
  - Network never approaches 20% of a 2 Gbps shape.

The days that looked busy were days someone was pushing commits, because each
deploy rebuilds the image. Development activity is not a property of the
service, so it cannot be what keeps the instance alive.

Memory is the right lever of the three. It is a level rather than a rate, so it
holds regardless of what the site is doing; there is no swap on this box, so
the pages stay resident once touched; and unlike burning CPU it costs nothing
to keep -- no heat, no contention with Postgres or the app.

The genuine fix is converting the tenancy to Pay As You Go, which exempts the
instance from reclamation entirely while leaving Always Free resources free.
That was declined deliberately (it needs a card on file). This is the fallback,
and it defends a real production site whose workload the policy's proxy metrics
simply do not capture.

Sized to land near 40%, well clear of the line rather than balanced on it.
Safety comes from the unit file rather than from here: MemoryMax caps it, and
OOMScoreAdjust=1000 means that if the kernel ever runs short this process is
killed first -- never Postgres, never the app.
"""

import sys
import time

PAGE = 4096


def main() -> int:
    mib = int(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 2560

    # A large bytearray is mmap'd anonymously and filled lazily, so allocating
    # it is not enough to make the pages resident -- and a reservation the
    # kernel has not actually handed out does not move the metric. Touching one
    # byte per page is what forces them in.
    buf = bytearray(mib * 1024 * 1024)
    for i in range(0, len(buf), PAGE):
        buf[i] = 1

    print(f"holding {mib} MiB resident", flush=True)

    # Nothing to do from here. The allocation is the whole job, and with no
    # swap configured the kernel cannot page it back out.
    while True:
        time.sleep(3600)
        # Keep a reference the interpreter cannot optimise away.
        if buf[0] != 1:
            return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
